  "content_description": "",
  "content_text_body": "When making decisions leaders sometimes forget that others don't have the same context or set of assumptions. The goal is to form a simple communication habit to make sure that your decisions about team goals are clearly understood by your team. \n\n### Restate the reasons\n\nWhen communicating a decision to your team, start by reminding everyone what the decision is intended to achieve. Why are you prioritizing these particular goals at this time? \n\n### Explain your process\n\nBe open about how you made the decision. What sources of information were used (e.g., stakeholder input, data)? Who were the backers and approvers of the decision? Who else contributed to it? What external opinions were sought? How long did it take?\n\nBeing transparent about decision making also allows the larger team to observe and provide input on the processes. \n\n### Be flexible and adjust\n\nBe clear about the assumptions and educated guesses you made while making your decision. Let your team know that you will adjust goals as conditions and priorities change, and that you rely on the team's feedback to let you know which goals are attainable.",
